[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, let me recall the cohomology of these spaces.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$H^*(\mathbb{C}P^n; \mathbb{Z}) = \mathbb{Z}[\alpha]/(\alpha^{n+1})$ where $|[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\alpha| = 2$. So $H^{2n}(\mathbb{C}P^n; \mathbb{Z}) = \mathbb{Z}$, generated by [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$\alpha^n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$H^*(S^{2n}; \mathbb{Z}) = \mathbb{Z}$ in degrees $0$ and $2n$, zero otherwise. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mSo $H^{2n}(S^{2n}; \mathbb{Z}) = \mathbb{Z}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBoth are $\mathbb{Z}$, so an isomorphism between them means $f^*$ sends a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgenerator to $\pm$ a generator, i.e., $f^*(\alpha^n) = \pm$ generator of $H^{2n}[0m[38;2;124;124;124m(S^{2n})$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, the key question: when does such a map exist?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe map $f: S^{2n} \to \mathbb{C}P^n$ has degree (in some sense) when we look at[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe top cohomology. The condition is that $f^*: H^{2n}(\mathbb{C}P^n) \to H^{2n}[0m[38;2;124;124;124m(S^{2n})$ is an isomorphism, meaning $f$ has degree $\pm 1$ with respect to the [0m[38;2;124;124;124mtop cohomology.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, there's a classical result. Consider the inclusion $\mathbb{C}P^n [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\hookrightarrow \mathbb{C}P^\infty = K(\mathbb{Z}, 2)$. The generator $\alpha [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\in H^2(\mathbb{C}P^n)$ is the pullback of the fundamental class of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$K(\mathbb{Z}, 2)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA map $f: S^{2n} \to \mathbb{C}P^n$ that induces an isomorphism on $H^{2n}$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwould mean that $f^*(\alpha^n) = \pm$ generator.[0m

