[38;2;124;124;124mBut $f^*\alpha \in H^2(S^{2n}) = 0$ for $n \geq 2$ (since $S^{2n}$ has no [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcohomology in degree 2 when $n \geq 2$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, that's the key point! For $n \geq 2$, $H^2(S^{2n}; \mathbb{Z}) = 0$, so [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$f^*(\alpha) = 0$, hence $f^*(\alpha^n) = (f^*\alpha)^n = 0$. This means $f^*$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcannot be an isomorphism on $H^{2n}$ for $n \geq 2$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $n = 1$: $S^2 \to \mathbb{C}P^1 \cong S^2$. Here $H^2(S^2) = \mathbb{Z}$ and[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$H^2(\mathbb{C}P^1) = \mathbb{Z}$, and $\alpha^1 = \alpha$ is the generator. A [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmap $f: S^2 \to S^2$ of degree $\pm 1$ (e.g., the identity) induces an [0m
